    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0209" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/14685: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-01-25T00:00:00-04:00">January 25,
                            1943—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>137. Reference Department’s telegram to Chungking, no. 58, March 13, 1941<anchor
                        n="6" xml:id="d154-f1"/> and Embassy’s telegram no. 114, March 26,
                        1941.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d154-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>A report has reached us, presumably based on statements made by Edgar Snow<anchor
                        n="8" xml:id="d154-f3"/> and Chou En-lai, to the effect that, due to such
                    factors I as (1) deteriorating economic condition of the people in areas under
                    the control of the National Government, (2) deterioration of the National
                    Government’s armies resulting from economic and financial situation and from the
                    static condition of China’s warfare against Japan, and (3) the growing strength
                    of the Chinese communist armies through defections from National Government
                    armies and sale of arms to communist forces by soldiers of the National
                    Government armies, the National Government may in the near future take
                    repressive steps against the communists which might result in outbreak of civil
                    war.</p>
                <p>The Department would appreciate your views, without your making specific
                    inquiries outside the Embassy, in regard to the foregoing report.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
